  "surprise": "P01/P03 under Muon: aliasing logical blocks 1-3 of the 5-block 081-claude incumbent (A-B-B-B-C, 3 unique blocks, 2,420,480 vs 4,000,000 params, same FLOPs) scored 363/306/338 on seeds 0/1/2 versus 433/356/422: exact means 335.67 versus 403.67, -68.0, worse on every seed (paired -70/-50/-84); inside the registered -90..+30 range but far below its -30 center. Discard. Surprise: pretraining did not register the 39% capacity cut at all. Steps 4875/4857/4899 (081: 4682/4732/4769; +4%, plausibly from 13 instead of 21 Muon matrices), final train 0.060/0.060/0.061 (predicted 0.07-0.08; 081 ~0.061), final val 0.0970/0.0921/0.0921 (081: 0.0950/0.0912/0.0964), held-out greedy 0.987/0.986/0.985 (081: 0.988/0.984/0.990). The loss appeared only in length extrapolation: round-1 RL solved 128/95/244 versus 187/235/306 and final RL-solved 773/721/816 versus 864/799/898, so the shared core fits <=6-line data equally well but starts expert iteration on >=7-line targets weaker and never catches up. That confirms the data-limited premise (unique capacity is not needed for in-distribution loss) but refutes the regularization payoff: the untied middle blocks carry something that matters for extrapolation and is invisible to val loss. Third negative sharing result (014 A-B-A-B -73, 028 A-B-B-D -18 pre-Muon). A further P01 test should add logical applications to the untied 5 blocks (extra depth at the cost of steps) rather than remove unique parameters at fixed compute. Total secs 1014/1168/1115, all under the cap.",
